  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWest to northwest flow aloft with one weak shortwave entering\nnorthwestern ND and another, stronger wave further upstream \nover the Northern Rockies. That stronger shortwave will move out\ninto the Plains on Saturday, pushing a surface trough axis into\nthe Red River Valley. That shortwave moves quickly off to the\nGreat Lakes, leaving northwesterly flow and surface high\npressure for the later part of the weekend. West to\nnorthwesterly flow aloft continues into the upcoming work week.\nSome weak embedded shortwaves are not completely out of the \nquestion, but overall the pattern seems to be drying out as we \nhead into the first full week of October. Some of the ensemble \nmembers are pretty amplified with upper ridging setting up over \nor just west of the Plains, but overall ECMWF EFI shows \ntemperature signals just slightly on the warm side of average \ntowards the middle and end of next week. \n\n...Light rain tonight/tomorrow then dry and somewhat warmer...\n\nMost of the high resolution models bring two rounds of light\nrain, the first late this afternoon and tonight with the warm\nair advection and weak lead shortwave, and the second round with\nthe stronger shortwave and surface trough axis. Regional radar\nand surface obs show some of the CAMs are already overdoing the\nfirst round, so not expecting much more than a few sprinkles at\nbest. Better chances for some light rain later tonight into\ntomorrow. Probabilities of at least some measurable\nprecipitation are high along and north of Highway 200, and there\nis even some 30 to 40 percent chances of seeing more than a\nquarter of an inch in northwestern MN. Probabilities for\nmeasurable rain drop off quite a bit after tomorrow. There is\nover 70 percent probability of 36 degrees or less in portions of\nour northern counties as well as our cold spots Monday morning.\nHowever, warming trend after that with temps returning to\nslightly above average values. There is even some low (20\npercent) chances for far southeastern ND to see 80 degrees,\nalthough that looks like a bit of an outlier.",
